xnu 0111: leave CNTV unmasked and print the retained PPI state

The 0110 hook-off boot read CNTV_CTL_EL0 as 0x7 across 17 sampler snapshots:
ENABLE=1, IMASK=1, ISTATUS=1, with CNTVCT advancing past the unchanged CVAL.
The timer condition was expired but masked. Bit 1 is architecturally IMASK,
not an EL1 interrupt-enable bit, so clear it and let the PPI reach the GIC.
The first 0110 PPI snapshot was taken before the console was ready and never
appeared in the ring. Cache its read-only GICR values during early setup and
print them later from the post-VM GIC dump; do not reread the faulting GICR
register page after the VM remap. This boot tests whether periodic kernel
waits and the launchd handoff recover with the architectural mask value.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
@@ -2272,18 +2272,13 @@
 {
 #if defined(MONDRIAN)
 	/*
-	 * The virtual timer interrupt has to be unmasked for EL1 explicitly.
-	 * On sweet the kernel gets the timer from the bootloader with no
-	 * hypervisor in between, and there the EL0 view of CNTV_CTL_EL0 is
-	 * all there is: bit 1 is IMASKED there, and XNU leaves it clear, so
-	 * the interrupt goes out. This phone has the hypervisor (QHEE) in
-	 * between, and with EL2 present the same encoding is the EL1 view,
-	 * where bit 1 is EL1IRQEN instead: with XNU's value the timer
-	 * interrupt is never enabled to EL1, so no timeout ever expires (the
-	 * boot then waits forever in IOKitInitializeTime() for an RTC that
-	 * this phone does not have).
+	 * CNTV_CTL_EL0 bit 1 is IMASK on the architectural register. The
+	 * 0110 readback showed 0x7 (ENABLE | IMASK | ISTATUS) while CNTVCT
+	 * advanced beyond CVAL: the compare was expired but masked. Clear
+	 * IMASK so the level-sensitive PPI can reach EL1. Keep the GIC PPI
+	 * snapshots in 0110 to confirm delivery on the next boot.
 	 */
-	uint64_t cntvctl = CNTV_CTL_EL0_ENABLE | (1 << 1); /* EL1IRQEN with EL2 present */
+	uint64_t cntvctl = CNTV_CTL_EL0_ENABLE;
 #else
 	uint64_t cntvctl = CNTV_CTL_EL0_ENABLE; /* One wants to use 32 bits, but "mrs" prefers it this way */
 #endif
